arch butterfly's body temperature is <math alttext=\"51.3 degree upper F\"><mn>51.3</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo><mtext>F</mtext></math>, what is the minimum increase needed in its body temperature, in <math alttext=\"degree upper F\"><mo>&#176;</mo><mtext>F</mtext></math>, so that it can fly?</p>","externalid":"5033c9a1-3f55-43c6-abad-0ff80dc97762","templateid":"35779190-020e-4dbe-99c4-0f604b930343","vaultid":"cf82a936-c58b-4646-a8ee-bf81907f0450","type":"mcq","answerOptions":[{"id":"cfc6a34d-3dea-419d-9121-c1d0bdb302b6","content":"<p><math alttext=\"1.3\"><mn>1.3</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"4aa0f1aa-aa2a-4fc0-b2a0-76a62ab3886c","content":"<p><math alttext=\"3.7\"><mn>3.7</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"1f93b930-53c0-4fa2-9f4c-ddbf10cd63bc","content":"<p><math alttext=\"5.0\"><mn>5.0</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"802c3a71-cf18-436d-b0dd-5169a7512e8b","content":"<p><math alttext=\"6.3\"><mn>6.3</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"correct_answer":["B"]},"createDate":1691007959820,"updateDate":1691007959820}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959820, 1691007959820),
    ($SATQ$b75f7812$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$05401-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.E.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Linear inequalities in one or two variables$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">Maria plans to rent a boat. The boat rental costs $60 per hour, and she will also have to pay for a water safety course that costs $10. Maria wants to spend no more than $280 for the rental and the course. If the boat rental is available only for a whole number of hours, what is the maximum number of hours for which Maria can rent the boat?</p>
$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>The correct answer is 4. The equation <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b75f7812/img_000_93998478.png" alt="60 h plus 10, is less than or equal to 280"></span>, where <span class="italic">h</span> is the number of hours the boat has been rented, can be written to represent the situation. Subtracting 10 from both sides and then dividing by 60 yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b75f7812/img_001_7bb995b7.png" alt="h is less than or equal to 4 point 5"></span>. Since the boat can be rented only for whole numbers of hours, the maximum number of hours for which Maria can rent the boat is 4.</p>
$SATQ$, true, 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$SPR$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"b75f7812","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"05401-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.E.","skill_desc":"Linear inequalities in one or two variables","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">Maria plans to rent a boat. The boat rental costs $60 per hour, and she will also have to pay for a water safety course that costs $10. Maria wants to spend no more than $280 for the rental and the course. If the boat rental is available only for a whole number of hours, what is the maximum number of hours for which Maria can rent the boat?</p>\n","answerOptions":null,"keys":null,"correct_answer":["4"],"rationale":"<p>The correct answer is 4. The equation <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b75f7812/img_000_93998478.png\" alt=\"60 h plus 10, is less than or equal to 280\"></span>, where <span class=\"italic\">h</span> is the number of hours the boat has been rented, can be written to represent the situation. Subtracting 10 from both sides and then dividing by 60 yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b75f7812/img_001_7bb995b7.png\" alt=\"h is less than or equal to 4 point 5\"></span>. Since the boat can be rented only for whole numbers of hours, the maximum number of hours for which Maria can rent the boat is 4.</p>\n","answer_style":"SPR","section":"Math","image_count":2,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959629,"pPcc":"SAT#H","questionId":"b75f7812","skill_cd":"H.E.","score_band_range_cd":3,"skill_desc":"Linear inequalities in one or two variables","createDate":1691007959629,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Algebra","ibn":"05401-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"H","uId":"e936101c-aa8d-466f-91f6-6eb3a0d87826","difficulty":"E"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"05401-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">Maria plans to rent a boat. The boat rental costs $60 per hour, and she will also have to pay for a water safety course that costs $10. Maria wants to spend no more than $280 for the rental and the course. If the boat rental is available only for a whole number of hours, what is the maximum number of hours for which Maria can rent the boat?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"SPR","rationale":"<p>The correct answer is 4. The equation <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"60 h plus 10, is less than or equal to 280\"></span>, where <span class=\"italic\">h</span> is the number of hours the boat has been rented, can be written to represent the situation. Subtracting 10 from both sides and then dividing by 60 yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"h is less than or equal to 4 point 5\"></span>. Since the boat can be rented only for whole numbers of hours, the maximum number of hours for which Maria can rent the boat is 4.</p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959629,"updateDate":1691007959629,"correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale":true}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959629, 1691007959629),
    ($SATQ$b78cd5df$SATQ$, $SATQ$a19c43f7-f093-4542-805a-9d67d7af0efa$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.E.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Linear inequalities in one or two variables$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">An event planner is planning a party. It costs the event planner a onetime fee of <math alttext="dollar sign 35"><mo>$</mo><mn>35</mn>
</math> to rent the venue and <math alttext="dollar sign 10.25"><mo>$</mo><mn>10.25</mn>
</math> per attendee. The event planner has a budget of <math alttext="dollar sign 300"><mo>$</mo><mn>300</mn>
</math>. What is the greatest number of attendees possible without exceeding the budget?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["25"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["25"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math alttext="25"><mn>25</mn>
</math>. The total cost of the party is found by adding the onetime fee of the venue to the cost per attendee times the number of attendees. Let <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> be the number of attendees. The expression <math alttext="35 plus 10.25 x"><mn>35</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10.25</mn><mi>x</mi></math> thus represents the total cost of the party. It's given that the budget is <math alttext="dollar sign 300"><mo>$</mo><mn>300</mn></math>, so this situation can be represented by the inequality <math alttext="35 plus 10.25 x less than or equals 300"><mn>35</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10.25</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&le;</mo><mn>300</mn></math>. Subtracting <math alttext="35"><mn>35</mn>
</math> from both sides of this inequality gives <math alttext="10.25 x less than or equals 265"><mn>10.25</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&le;</mo><mn>265</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this inequality by <math alttext="10.25"><mn>10.25</mn>
</math> results in approximately <math alttext="x less than or equals 25.854"><mi>x</mi><mo>&le;</mo><mn>25.854</mn></math>. Since the question is stated in terms of attendees, rounding <math alttext="25.854"><mn>25.854</mn>
</math> down to the greatest whole number gives the greatest number of attendees possible, which is <math alttext="25"><mn>25</mn>
</math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b78cd5df","external_id":"a19c43f7-f093-4542-805a-9d67d7af0efa","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"7012bedf-1125-48da-bd24-d6d3794df5f8","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.E.","skill_desc":"Linear inequalities in one or two variables","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">An event planner is planning a party. It costs the event planner a onetime fee of <math alttext=\"dollar sign 35\"><mo>$</mo><mn>35</mn>\n</math> to rent the venue and <math alttext=\"dollar sign 10.25\"><mo>$</mo><mn>10.25</mn>\n</math> per attendee. The event planner has a budget of <math alttext=\"dollar sign 300\"><mo>$</mo><mn>300</mn>\n</math>. What is the greatest number of attendees possible without exceeding the budget?</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["25"],"correct_answer":["25"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"25\"><mn>25</mn>\n</math>. The total cost of the party is found by adding the onetime fee of the venue to the cost per attendee times the number of attendees. Let <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> be the number of attendees. The expression <math alttext=\"35 plus 10.25 x\"><mn>35</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10.25</mn><mi>x</mi></math> thus represents the total cost of the party. It's given that the budget is <math alttext=\"dollar sign 300\"><mo>$</mo><mn>300</mn></math>, so this situation can be represented by the inequality <math alttext=\"35 plus 10.25 x less than or equals 300\"><mn>35</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10.25</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&le;</mo><mn>300</mn></math>. Subtracting <math alttext=\"35\"><mn>35</mn>\n</math> from both sides of this inequality gives <math alttext=\"10.25 x less than or equals 265\"><mn>10.25</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&le;</mo><mn>265</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this inequality by <math alttext=\"10.25\"><mn>10.25</mn>\n</math> results in approximately <math alttext=\"x less than or equals 25.854\"><mi>x</mi><mo>&le;</mo><mn>25.854</mn></math>. Since the question is stated in terms of attendees, rounding <math alttext=\"25.854\"><mn>25.854</mn>\n</math> down to the greatest whole number gives the greatest number of attendees possible, which is <math alttext=\"25\"><mn>25</mn>\n</math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1743430554984,"pPcc":"SAT#H","questionId":"b78cd5df","skill_cd":"H.E.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Linear inequalities in one or two variables","createDate":1743430554984,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Algebra","ibn":"","external_id":"a19c43f7-f093-4542-805a-9d67d7af0efa","primary_class_cd":"H","uId":"7012bedf-1125-48da-bd24-d6d3794df5f8","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"type":"spr","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">An event planner is planning a party. It costs the event planner a onetime fee of <math alttext=\"dollar sign 35\"><mo>$</mo><mn>35</mn>\n</math> to rent the venue and <math alttext=\"dollar sign 10.25\"><mo>$</mo><mn>10.25</mn>\n</math> per attendee. The event planner has a budget of <math alttext=\"dollar sign 300\"><mo>$</mo><mn>300</mn>\n</math>. What is the greatest number of attendees possible without exceeding the budget?</p>","keys":["25"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"25\"><mn>25</mn>\n</math>. The total cost of the party is found by adding the onetime fee of the venue to the cost per attendee times the number of attendees. Let <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> be the number of attendees. The expression <math alttext=\"35 plus 10.25 x\"><mn>35</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10.25</mn><mi>x</mi></math> thus represents the total cost of the party. It's given that the budget is <math alttext=\"dollar sign 300\"><mo>$</mo><mn>300</mn></math>, so this situation can be represented by the inequality <math alttext=\"35 plus 10.25 x less than or equals 300\"><mn>35</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10.25</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&le;</mo><mn>300</mn></math>. Subtracting <math alttext=\"35\"><mn>35</mn>\n</math> from both sides of this inequality gives <math alttext=\"10.25 x less than or equals 265\"><mn>10.25</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&le;</mo><mn>265</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this inequality by <math alttext=\"10.25\"><mn>10.25</mn>\n</math> results in approximately <math alttext=\"x less than or equals 25.854\"><mi>x</mi><mo>&le;</mo><mn>25.854</mn></math>. Since the question is stated in terms of attendees, rounding <math alttext=\"25.854\"><mn>25.854</mn>\n</math> down to the greatest whole number gives the greatest number of attendees possible, which is <math alttext=\"25\"><mn>25</mn>\n</math>.</p>","externalid":"a19c43f7-f093-4542-805a-9d67d7af0efa","answerOptions":[],"correct_answer":["25"]},"createDate":1743430554984,"updateDate":1743430554984}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430554984, 1743430554984),
    ($SATQ$b7e6394d$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$05364-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Linear equations in one variable$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">Alan drives an average of 100&nbsp;miles each week. His car can travel an average of 25&nbsp;miles per gallon of gasoline. Alan would like to reduce his weekly expenditure on gasoline by $5. Assuming gasoline costs $4&nbsp;per gallon, which equation can Alan use to determine how many fewer average miles, <span class="italic">m</span>, he should drive each week?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b7e6394d/img_000_1564d425.png\" alt=\"the fraction 25 over 4, end fraction, times m, equals, 95\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b7e6394d/img_001_ba3246e1.png\" alt=\"the fraction 25 over 4, end fraction, times m, equals, 5\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b7e6394d/img_002_cdae0d0d.png\" alt=\"the fraction 4 over 25, end fraction, times m, equals, 95\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b7e6394d/img_003_96bf7ef8.png\" alt=\"the fraction 4 over 25, end fraction, times m, equals, 5\"></span></span></p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is correct. Since gasoline costs $4 per gallon, and since Alan&rsquo;s car travels an average of 25 miles per gallon, the expression <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b7e6394d/img_004_2ff0f584.png" alt="4 over 25"></span> gives the cost, in dollars per mile, to drive the car. Multiplying <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b7e6394d/img_005_2ff0f584.png" alt="4 over 25"></span> by <span class="italic">m</span> gives the cost for Alan to drive<span class="italic"> m </span>miles in his car. Alan wants to reduce his weekly spending by $5, so setting <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b7e6394d/img_006_2ff0f584.png" alt="4 over 25"></span><span class="italic">m</span> equal to 5 gives the number of miles, <span class="italic">m</span>, by which he must reduce his driving.<p>Choices A, B, and C are incorrect. Choices A and B transpose the numerator and the denominator in the fraction. The fraction <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b7e6394d/img_007_d0a73228.png" alt="25 over 4"></span> would result in the unit miles per dollar, but the question requires a unit of dollars per mile. Choices A and C set the expression equal to 95 instead of 5, a mistake that may result from a misconception that Alan wants to reduce his driving by 5 miles each week; instead, the question says he wants to reduce his weekly expenditure by $5.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>
